# Batch 35 (2026-11-22): Neanderthal burials, Mesolithic and submerged Neolithic sites, Iron Age temples and graves, steppe and Silk Road peoples, al-Andalus;
# early-modern and 20th-century turning points (thin until now); later legends, contactees and modern scientific controversies
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None,statusNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  if statusNote:d['statusNote']=statusNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: deep and ancient worlds
E+=[
X('shanidar-flower-burial','Shanidar Cave: the Neanderthal "flower burial" and the man who was cared for',H,'confirmed',2,-70000,['archaeological','fossil','pollen analysis'],
 "Shanidar Cave lies on Bradost Mountain in the Zagros range of Iraqi Kurdistan. Between 1957 and 1961 the American archaeologist Ralph Solecki and his team uncovered the remains of nine Neanderthals there, and further individuals were identified later, including Shanidar Z, announced in 2020 and dated to more than 70,000 years ago. Shanidar 1, an older man nicknamed 'Nandy', had a withered, fractured right arm, a crushed eye socket and severe hearing loss, yet lived with these injuries for a long time. Shanidar 4 became famous as the 'flower burial' after soil samples analysed by Arlette Leroi-Gourhan contained clumps of pollen from flowering plants, which Solecki interpreted as flowers deliberately laid in the grave.",
 [('Flowers or burrowing animals?','Many researchers now think the pollen clumps were brought in by animals, especially the burrowing Persian jird (a gerbil-like rodent) whose burrows were found nearby, or by nesting bees; Paul Pettitt wrote that deliberate placement of flowers had been convincingly eliminated, while still regarding the Shanidar burials as deliberate mortuary behaviour.'),
  ('Neanderthal compassion','Shanidar 1\'s long survival with disabling injuries is widely read as evidence that others in his group looked after him, a key part of the shift from seeing Neanderthals as brutish to seeing them as caring; how far this reflects empathy as modern humans understand it is debated.')],
 ['doi:10.15184/aqy.2019.207','wiki:Shanidar_Cave'],img='Shanidar Cave',dateNote='Burials roughly 70,000-45,000 years ago; excavated 1951-1961 and again since 2015',statusNote='burials confirmed; "flower" interpretation disputed',
 changed="In 1971 Solecki's book 'Shanidar: The First Flower People' made the flower burial famous as a sign of Neanderthal spirituality; from the 1990s onward the rodent and bee explanations gained ground; new excavations from 2015 found Shanidar Z close to the Shanidar 4 grave."),
X('star-carr','Star Carr: Mesolithic antler headdresses and Britain\'s oldest house',H,'confirmed',2,-9000,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "Star Carr is a Mesolithic site near Scarborough in North Yorkshire, England, on the edge of a vanished lake, occupied about 11,000 years ago when Britain was still joined to continental Europe. Noticed by the amateur archaeologist John Moore in 1947, with excavations beginning in 1948 and the classic campaign led by Grahame Clark from 1949 to 1951, it is regarded as the most important Mesolithic site in Britain. Waterlogged peat preserved rare organic finds: about 200 barbed points made of red deer antler and 21 red deer skull-caps with the antlers still attached, carefully thinned and perforated. New excavations led by Nicky Milner, Chantal Conneller and Barry Taylor also found the remains of a circular post-built structure, described as Britain's oldest known house, and a large wooden platform at the lake edge.",
 [('Shamans\' masks or hunting disguises?','The perforated deer frontlets are often interpreted as headdresses worn in rituals, possibly by shamans, by analogy with later Siberian and northern peoples; others have proposed they were hunting disguises used to stalk deer. Both ideas are interpretations; the objects themselves cannot show how they were used.'),
  ('A disappearing site','The excavators warned that changes in the water table were causing the peat to dry and acidify, so that bone and antler still in the ground were rapidly decaying; the risk of losing the evidence drove the renewed digging.')],
 ['doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0152136','wiki:Star_Carr'],img='File:Star Carr Antler Mask in the British Museum.jpg',end=-8500),
X('atlit-yam','Atlit Yam: a 9,000-year-old village under the Mediterranean',H,'confirmed',2,-6900,['archaeological','underwater archaeology','genetic'],
 "Atlit Yam is a submerged Neolithic village lying about 300–400 metres off the coast of Atlit, south of Haifa in Israel, at depths of roughly 8–12 metres. It was spotted in 1984 by the marine archaeologist Ehud Galili while surveying for shipwrecks. Dating to the late 7th and early 6th millennia BCE, it shows a community combining farming, herding and fishing on the Levantine coast. Divers have recorded stone houses, a dry-stone well about 5.5 metres deep, at least 90 human burials and a semicircle of large standing stones with cup marks set around a spring. Bones of a woman and child found there produced in 2008 the earliest DNA-confirmed cases of human tuberculosis.",
 [('Slow rise or sudden wave?','Galili proposed that the village was abandoned gradually as rising sea levels salinised its well, which was later used as a rubbish pit. A study led by Maria Pareschi modelled a large tsunami from a collapse of Mount Etna\'s flank about 8,500 years ago, and some researchers have linked the abandonment of Atlit Yam to such a wave; the idea is debated.'),
  ('Sunken-city legends','Submerged prehistoric sites like Atlit Yam are sometimes cited in support of lost-civilisation ideas about drowned coasts; archaeologists see them as ordinary Neolithic villages flooded by the well-documented rise in sea level after the Ice Age.')],
 ['doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0003426','wiki:Atlit_Yam'],img='Atlit Yam',end=-6300,dateNote='Late 7th to early 6th millennium BCE'),
X('ain-dara-footprints','The giant footprints of Ain Dara temple',H,'confirmed',2,-1300,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Ain Dara is an archaeological site about 67 km northwest of Aleppo in Syria, best known for an Iron Age Syro-Hittite temple excavated by Ali Abu Assaf after a colossal basalt lion was found by chance in 1955. The excavator dated its use to about 1300–740 BCE. Its walls were lined with reliefs of lions and sphinxes, and its floor was carved with a pair of bare footprints each about a metre long, followed by a single footprint and another on the threshold, as if a giant were striding into the building. The temple's plan, with a portico, middle room and main hall, closely resembles the description of Solomon's Temple in the Hebrew Bible. In January 2018 the temple was badly damaged during the Turkish military operation in Afrin.",
 [('The footprints of a god','Scholars generally interpret the footprints as a symbol of the deity, perhaps the storm god Hadad or the goddess Ishtar, entering his or her house; John Monson compared them to Ezekiel 43:7, where the temple is called "the place of the soles of my feet".'),
  ('Giants and Nephilim','Some writers on ancient giants or ancient astronauts point out that a being with such feet would be about 20 m tall and treat the prints as a memory of real giants; this is speculative and not supported by any other evidence at the site.'),
  ('Solomon\'s Temple','The similarities with the biblical temple are widely discussed; they are used by some as evidence that the biblical description reflects a real Iron Age building type, and by others simply as a regional architectural tradition.')],
 ['wiki:Ain_Dara_(archaeological_site)'],img='Ain Dara (archaeological site)',end=-740),
X('vix-grave','The Lady of Vix and the largest bronze vessel of the ancient West',H,'confirmed',2,-470,['archaeological'],
 "The Vix Grave is a burial mound near the village of Vix in northern Burgundy, France, part of a Celtic complex beneath the hill fort of Mont Lassois. It was discovered by Maurice Moisson and excavated with René Joffroy in early 1953. The undisturbed grave of a woman, the 'Lady of Vix', buried around 470 BCE, held a dismantled four-wheeled wagon, a gold torc of about 480 grams and many imports from the Mediterranean: Etruscan and Athenian drinking vessels and the Vix krater, a bronze wine-mixing vessel 1.64 m tall, weighing about 208 kg and holding about 1,100 litres. It is the largest known metal vessel from Western classical antiquity and was probably shipped in pieces, carrying Greek letters as assembly marks.",
 [('A princess, a priestess or a ruler?','Because the burial contains jewellery and no weapons, the dead person has been interpreted as a woman. Whether she was a ruler in her own right, a priestess, or the relative of a male chief is debated; her status shows that women could receive the richest burials of the age.'),
  ('Gift or trade?','The krater may have been a diplomatic gift from Greek traders seeking access to tin or other goods moving along the Seine, or a prestige purchase; the find shows how far Mediterranean luxury goods reached into Iron Age Gaul.')],
 ['wiki:Vix_Grave','wiki:Mont_Lassois'],img='File:Chatillon-sur-Seine - Musée du Pays chatillonnais - Cratère de Vix - 01 (cropped).jpg'),
X('xiongnu','The Xiongnu: the steppe empire that faced Han China, and the Hun question',H,'confirmed',2,-209,['textual','archaeological','genetic'],
 "The Xiongnu were a confederation of nomadic peoples of the eastern Eurasian steppe, known mainly from Chinese sources such as Sima Qian. In 209 BCE Modu Chanyu became their leader and built a steppe empire centred on the Mongolian Plateau. In 200 BCE he surrounded the first Han emperor, Gaozu, at Baideng, after which the Han paid tribute and sent princesses in marriage (the heqin policy). Under Emperor Wu the Han went on the offensive. The confederation split into northern and southern parts in 48 CE; in 89 CE a Han army under Dou Xian defeated the Northern Xiongnu, and the historian Ban Gu composed a victory inscription that was carved on a cliff in Mongolia and rediscovered there in 2017. The Southern Xiongnu settled within China and were gradually absorbed.",
 [('Ancestors of the Huns?','The 18th-century French historian Joseph de Guignes proposed that the Xiongnu were the ancestors of the Huns who reached Europe in the 4th century. The idea is now accepted by many scholars, supported by Sogdian letters that use the same name for both and by some ancient DNA, but it is not a consensus; others think the Huns were a different confederation that adopted a prestigious name.'),
  ('Who were they?','The Xiongnu appear to have been multi-ethnic. Their language is unknown, and proposals include Turkic, Mongolic, Iranian and Yeniseian; genetic studies of Xiongnu burials show a mix of eastern and western Eurasian ancestry.')],
 ['wiki:Xiongnu','wiki:Inscription_of_Yanran'],img='File:Met, china, two belt buckles with standing oxes, nwchina, Xiongnu culture, 3-01st century BC.jpg',end=150,dateNote='c. 209 BCE - 2nd century CE'),
X('kushan-kanishka','The Kushan Empire and Kanishka, the king rediscovered on a field stone',H,'confirmed',2,30,['textual','archaeological','numismatic'],
 "The Kushan Empire (about 30–375 CE) was founded by the Kushans, one branch of the Yuezhi, nomads who had migrated from northwestern China into Bactria. At its height it covered much of present-day Afghanistan, Pakistan and northern India and parts of Central Asia, controlling a crossroads of trade between Rome, Persia, India and China. Its rulers used Greek and then the Bactrian language, and its coins show Greek, Iranian, Hindu and Buddhist deities side by side. Kanishka the Great, in the 2nd century CE, is remembered in Buddhist tradition as a great patron; Gandharan art flourished, and monks from the region helped carry Buddhism to China. In 1993 a stone inscription in Bactrian found at Rabatak in Afghanistan, read by Nicholas Sims-Williams and Joe Cribb, gave Kanishka's genealogy back to Kujula Kadphises.",
 [('Forgotten and found','Much of the Kushan dynastic history was reconstructed from coins and scattered inscriptions; the Rabatak inscription, saved after being found by Afghan workers and photographed by aid workers during the civil war, clarified the sequence of kings and suggested Kushan rule reached further into India than once thought.'),
  ('Dating disputes','The year Kanishka came to the throne has been debated for over a century, with proposals spanning more than 200 years; many historians now favour about 127 CE, but this is not universally agreed.')],
 ['wiki:Kushan_Empire','wiki:Kanishka','wiki:Rabatak_inscription'],img='Kanishka',end=375),
X('sogdian-merchants','The Sogdians: merchants of the Silk Road and a letter from an abandoned wife',H,'confirmed',2,313,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Sogdia, between the Amu Darya and Syr Darya rivers in present-day Uzbekistan and Tajikistan, was a land of city-states centred on Samarkand that was never politically united. Its people spoke an Eastern Iranian language, Sogdian, which became a lingua franca along the Silk Road; their merchants and diplomats travelled from the Byzantine Empire to China, and many settled in China, rising to prominence under the Tang. In 1907 Aurel Stein found the 'Ancient Letters', five Sogdian documents, in an abandoned watchtower near Dunhuang. One, dated to about 313 CE, reports that Luoyang had been sacked and its Sogdian and Indian residents had died of famine; another was written by a woman, Miwnay, abandoned in China by her husband, who wrote that she would rather have married a dog or a pig.",
 [('Middlemen of the world','Historians such as Étienne de la Vaissière and Valerie Hansen see the Sogdians as the most important trading diaspora of the Silk Road between the 4th and 8th centuries; their role faded after the An Lushan rebellion (led by a man of Sogdian descent) and the Islamic conquest of Central Asia.'),
  ('A language that survives','Sogdian itself is no longer spoken, but Yaghnobi, spoken by a small community in Tajikistan, descends from one of its dialects.')],
 ['wiki:Sogdia','wiki:Sogdian_language'],img='File:Afrasiab - details from The Ambassadors\' Painting 3 - great procession.JPG',dateNote='Ancient Letters c. 313 CE; Sogdian trade 4th-8th centuries'),
X('al-andalus-cordoba','Al-Andalus and Córdoba: the golden age of tolerance, or a myth?',H,'disputed',2,711,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In 711 an Umayyad army led by Tariq ibn Ziyad crossed from North Africa into the Iberian peninsula and within a few years conquered most of the Visigothic kingdom. The Muslim-ruled territory, called al-Andalus, became an independent emirate under Abd al-Rahman I in 756, and in 929 Abd al-Rahman III proclaimed the Caliphate of Córdoba. Córdoba became one of the largest and richest cities in Europe, with the Great Mosque, the palace-city of Madinat al-Zahra and a flourishing culture of science, philosophy and poetry in which Muslims, Jews and Christians all took part. The caliphate broke up into rival taifa kingdoms in 1031; Christian kingdoms expanded southward, and the last Muslim state, the Nasrid emirate of Granada, fell in 1492.",
 [('Convivencia','The Spanish historian Américo Castro used the term convivencia ("living together") for the coexistence of the three faiths, and writers such as María Rosa Menocal have described al-Andalus as a culture of tolerance.'),
  ('A myth of paradise?','Others, including David Nirenberg, Mark Cohen and Darío Fernández-Morera, argue that the idea is idealised; Cohen describes both the interfaith utopia and its opposite, a story of constant persecution, as myths. Non-Muslims lived as protected but subordinate dhimmis, and there were episodes of violence such as the 1066 Granada massacre.')],
 ['wiki:Al-Andalus','wiki:Caliphate_of_Córdoba','wiki:La_Convivencia'],img='Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba',end=1492),
X('boskop-man','Boskop Man: the "big-brained" lost race that never was',H,'debunked',2,-30000,['fossil','archaeological'],
 "The Boskop skull is a fossil human cranium found in 1913 by two Afrikaner farmers at Boskop in the former Transvaal, South Africa. Its large braincase led Robert Broom to describe a new species, Homo capensis, and for decades palaeontologists such as Broom and Raymond Dart assigned other large skulls to a 'Boskopoid' race said to have once lived in southern Africa. By the 1970s scientists had concluded that these skulls fall within the range of modern Khoe and San populations and do not form a separate type; the Boskopoid category is now seen as an artefact of the biases of the researchers who created it. The skull is undated but belongs to the Late Pleistocene or later.",
 [('A super-intelligent people?','In the book Big Brain (2008), the neuroscientists Gary Lynch and Richard Granger suggested that the Boskop people may have had exceptionally high intelligence; the anthropologist John Hawks strongly criticised this, noting that the Boskop type had been abandoned by physical anthropologists decades earlier.'),
  ('Race science in context','Historians of science link the Boskop idea to early 20th-century efforts to sort fossils and living people into races, and its abandonment to better samples and changing methods.')],
 ['wiki:Boskop_Man'],img='Boskop Man',dateNote='Skull found 1913; age uncertain (Late Pleistocene or Holocene)',
 changed="1913 skull found; 1918 Broom names Homo capensis; by the 1970s the Boskopoid type is abandoned; 2008 revived in a popular book and rebutted again."),
]
# --- part 2: the early-modern world, 1500-1850
E+=[
X('atlantic-slave-trade','The Atlantic slave trade: 12.5 million people taken across the ocean',H,'confirmed',1,1526,['textual','archaeological','database'],
 "The Atlantic slave trade carried enslaved Africans to the Americas from the early 16th century until the 1860s. Portuguese traders began buying captives on the West African coast in the 15th century; the first direct voyages from Africa to the Americas followed in the 1520s, and Portugal, Britain, France, Spain, the Netherlands, Denmark and traders in the Americas all took part. Most captives came from West and Central Africa and were bought from African merchants and states at coastal forts and markets. According to the SlaveVoyages database, built from records of more than 36,000 voyages, about 12.5 million people were embarked and about 10.7 million survived the Middle Passage. Most were taken to Brazil and the Caribbean to work on sugar and other plantations. Britain banned its trade in 1807 and the United States in 1808; the last known voyages reached Cuba in the 1860s, and Brazil abolished slavery in 1888.",
 [('Counting the dead and the living','The numbers have been revised as records were digitised; earlier estimates ranged from under 10 million to far higher figures. Historians agree on the order of magnitude but note that the deaths during capture and marches to the coast in Africa are poorly recorded and not included in the voyage totals.'),
  ('Causes and consequences','Historians debate how far the trade shaped Africa\'s later economic and political development, and how far profits from slavery fuelled European industrialisation (the thesis of Eric Williams\'s Capitalism and Slavery, 1944); these questions remain actively argued.')],
 ['url:https://www.slavevoyages.org/assessment/estimates|SlaveVoyages: Trans-Atlantic Slave Trade estimates|database','wiki:Atlantic_slave_trade'],img='File:Slave ship diagram.png',end=1867,dateNote='First direct Africa-Americas voyages c. 1525-26; last known voyages 1860s'),
X('potosi-silver','Potosí: the silver mountain that paid for an empire',H,'confirmed',2,1545,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In 1545 Diego Huallpa, an Indigenous prospector, is traditionally credited with finding silver at the Cerro Rico ('rich mountain') in what is now Bolivia. The mining town of Potosí, at over 4,000 metres one of the highest cities in the world, grew to be among the largest cities of its time. From the 1570s Viceroy Francisco de Toledo expanded the mita, a forced-labour draft adapted from an Inca system, which obliged Indigenous communities to send men to the mines, and introduced mercury amalgamation, using mercury from Huancavelica. Potosí's silver, minted into coins, flowed through Spain to the rest of Europe and across the Pacific via the Manila galleons to China, helping create a global trade in silver. The Cerro Rico became known as 'the mountain that eats men'.",
 [('How many died?','Figures of around eight million deaths are often repeated in popular works; other historians argue that the deaths in the Potosí mines numbered in the hundreds of thousands, and that the larger figure refers to population losses across the whole Viceroyalty of Peru, mostly from epidemics.'),
  ('Forced or wage labour?','Historians such as Noble David Cook stress that by 1603 most workers at Potosí were wage earners rather than mita conscripts; Peter Bakewell argues that the mita was more central, since many wage workers were conscripts on their weeks off or former conscripts who stayed on.')],
 ['wiki:Potosí','wiki:Cerro_Rico','wiki:Mit\'a'],img='Potosí',end=1825),
X('defenestration-thirty-years-war','The Thirty Years\' War: a window in Prague, a ruined Germany and the "Westphalian" myth',H,'confirmed',1,1618,['textual'],
 "On 23 May 1618 Protestant nobles in Prague threw two Catholic royal governors, Jaroslav Bořita of Martinice and Vilém Slavata, and their secretary out of a window of the castle, beginning the Bohemian revolt against the Habsburgs. The war that followed, fought mainly in the Holy Roman Empire between 1618 and 1648, drew in Spain, Sweden, Denmark, France and the Dutch Republic and became one of the most destructive in European history: an estimated 4.5 to 8 million people died from fighting, famine and disease, and some regions of Germany lost more than half their population. Religious division after the Reformation helped start it, but its scale owed much to power politics, especially the rivalry between France and the Habsburgs. The Peace of Westphalia, signed at Osnabrück and Münster in 1648, ended it.",
 [('Saved by angels or by dung?','All three men thrown from the window survived the fall of about 20 metres. Catholics said they had been saved by angels or by the Virgin Mary; later Protestant pamphlets claimed they had landed on a dung heap, a story unknown to contemporaries and probably invented to mock the miracle claims.'),
  ('The Westphalian myth','Westphalia is often said to have founded the modern system of sovereign states. Scholars such as Andreas Osiander argue this is a 19th- and 20th-century myth: the treaties were mainly about the constitution of the Holy Roman Empire and did not create sovereignty as later understood; others still see 1648 as a useful symbolic turning point.')],
 ['doi:10.1162/00208180151140577','wiki:Thirty_Years\'_War','wiki:Defenestrations_of_Prague'],img='File:VáclavBROŽÍK-Defenestrace.jpg',end=1648),
X('tulip-mania-1637','Tulip mania (1637): the first bubble, or a story that grew in the telling?',H,'disputed',2,1634,['textual','economic records'],
 "During the Dutch Golden Age, tulips, recently introduced from the Ottoman Empire, became fashionable luxury goods, and the most prized varieties had striped 'broken' petals caused, it was later found, by a virus. From about 1634 prices for bulb contracts rose steeply, and in February 1637 a single bulb of the rare Semper Augustus is reported to have sold for 10,000 guilders, more than many houses. In early February 1637 the market suddenly collapsed. Tulip mania is often called the first recorded speculative bubble and is a standard warning in finance books.",
 [('Mackay\'s madness of crowds','The famous version comes from Charles Mackay\'s Extraordinary Popular Delusions and the Madness of Crowds (1841), which described a whole nation gripped by speculation and ruined by the crash, with anecdotes such as a sailor who ate a priceless bulb mistaking it for an onion.'),
  ('A small, exaggerated crisis','The historian Anne Goldgar, studying archives for her book Tulipmania (2007), found that trading involved a fairly small group of merchants and artisans, that she could identify fewer than half a dozen people in financial trouble, and that most accounts rely on contemporary moralising pamphlets and much later copying. The economist Peter Garber argued that high prices for rare bulbs were not irrational; Earl Thompson linked the crash to a change in contract rules.')],
 ['wiki:Tulip_mania'],img='Tulip mania',end=1637,statusNote='price rise and crash confirmed; scale and impact disputed',
 changed="1637 crash and moralising pamphlets; 1841 Mackay popularises the mania; from the 1980s economists question it; 2007 Goldgar's archival study argues the crisis was small."),
X('great-fire-of-london-1666','The Great Fire of London (1666) and the search for someone to blame',H,'confirmed',1,1666,['textual','archaeological'],
 "The Great Fire of London began shortly after midnight on Sunday 2 September 1666 in a bakery in Pudding Lane and burned until 5 September. Driven by an east wind through closely packed timber houses, it gutted most of the medieval City of London inside the Roman wall, destroying about 13,200 houses, 87 parish churches and Old St Paul's Cathedral. The recorded death toll was very small, traditionally six, though some historians think it was higher. The city was rebuilt in brick and stone, and Christopher Wren designed the new St Paul's and many churches; the Monument near Pudding Lane, designed by Wren and Robert Hooke, was built between 1671 and 1677.",
 [('A foreign or Catholic plot?','During the fire, rumours that foreigners were setting fires led to violence against French and Dutch residents, as England was at war with the Dutch. A French watchmaker, Robert Hubert, confessed to starting it and was hanged at Tyburn in October 1666, despite doubts about his fitness to plead; it later emerged that he had been at sea and only reached London two days after the fire began; in 1681 an inscription blaming "Popish frenzy" was added to the Monument and was only removed in 1830.'),
  ('An accident','Official inquiries and most historians conclude the fire was an accident, made catastrophic by a dry summer, strong wind and slow decisions to demolish buildings as firebreaks.')],
 ['wiki:Great_Fire_of_London','wiki:Monument_to_the_Great_Fire_of_London'],img='Great Fire of London',end=1666),
X('south-sea-bubble-1720','The South Sea Bubble (1720): when Britain\'s debt became a craze',H,'confirmed',2,1720,['textual','economic records'],
 "The South Sea Company was founded in 1711 as a public-private partnership to take over and reduce the cost of Britain's national debt. In 1713 it received the Asiento, a monopoly contract to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America, and it transported over 34,000 people across the Atlantic, though the trade never made significant profits. In 1720 the company took on more of the national debt in exchange for shares, and its share price rose from about £128 in January to £1,000 in early August, amid a wave of speculation in other new companies, before collapsing to around £100 by the end of the year. Thousands of investors were ruined, and a parliamentary inquiry exposed bribery of ministers and officials; the Chancellor of the Exchequer, John Aislabie, was found guilty of corruption and imprisoned.",
 [('Newton\'s losses','A popular story says Isaac Newton lost a fortune and remarked that he could calculate the motions of heavenly bodies but not the madness of people. Newton did lose at least £10,000 in South Sea stock, but the remark comes second-hand, from Joseph Spence reporting what Lord Radnor told him, and the popular wording about stars is a later elaboration.'),
  ('Mania or rational gamble?','Economists debate whether the bubble was irrational mass speculation or a predictable result of the scheme\'s design and of insiders manipulating the price; the same year saw John Law\'s Mississippi bubble collapse in France.')],
 ['wiki:South_Sea_Company','wiki:Bubble_Act'],img='File:William Hogarth, The South Sea Scheme, 1721, NGA 30435.jpg',end=1720),
X('sakoku-closed-japan','Sakoku: Japan\'s "closed country" that was never quite closed',H,'confirmed',2,1639,['textual'],
 "Under the Tokugawa shogunate, a series of edicts issued between 1633 and 1639 under the shogun Tokugawa Iemitsu sharply restricted Japan's relations with the outside world. Most foreigners were barred, Portuguese traders were expelled after the Shimabara Rebellion, Christianity was banned, and ordinary Japanese could not leave the country on pain of death. Contact continued through four 'gateways': the Dutch East India Company post on the artificial island of Dejima and Chinese merchants at Nagasaki, Korea through the Tsushima domain, the Ryukyu kingdom through Satsuma, and the Ainu through the Matsumae domain. Western science reached Japan through rangaku ('Dutch learning'). The system ended after the American Commodore Matthew Perry arrived with his 'black ships' in 1853 and forced the Convention of Kanagawa in 1854.",
 [('A word coined later','The term sakoku was invented in 1801 by the translator Shizuki Tadao while translating the German traveller Engelbert Kaempfer\'s account of Japan; the shogunate itself did not use it. Historians such as Ronald Toby argue that the policy was a controlled foreign relations system rather than isolation.'),
  ('Isolation and its effects','Older accounts saw sakoku as leaving Japan technologically behind and stagnant; later historians emphasise the long peace, internal trade, urban culture and the steady flow of selected knowledge through Nagasaki, which helped Japan modernise rapidly after 1868.')],
 ['wiki:Sakoku','wiki:Dejima'],img='Dejima',end=1854),
X('cook-terra-australis-lono','Captain Cook, the missing southern continent and the Lono debate',H,'confirmed',2,1768,['textual'],
 "Since antiquity European geographers had imagined Terra Australis, a great southern continent thought to balance the land of the north, and it appeared on maps from the 15th to the 18th centuries. On his first voyage (1768–71) James Cook observed the 1769 transit of Venus in Tahiti and, under secret orders, searched for the southern continent, charting New Zealand and the east coast of Australia. On his second voyage (1772–75) he became the first known navigator to cross the Antarctic Circle and sailed around the world at high southern latitudes, showing that no large habitable southern continent existed; Antarctica itself was first sighted in 1820. On his third voyage he reached Hawaii, where he was killed at Kealakekua Bay on 14 February 1779.",
 [('Was Cook taken for a god?','Cook arrived during the Makahiki festival of the god Lono, and Hawaiians called him Lono and prostrated themselves. The anthropologist Marshall Sahlins argued that Hawaiians fitted Cook into their ritual cycle as Lono; Gananath Obeyesekere argued this was a European myth of natives worshipping white explorers. Anne Salmond suggests Cook was honoured as a high chief descended from Lono rather than as the god himself.'),
  ('Discovery or encounter?','Cook\'s voyages are now presented less as discoveries than as encounters with peoples who had long known these lands and seas, including the Raiatean navigator Tupaia, who sailed with Cook and drew a chart of the Pacific islands.')],
 ['wiki:Terra_Australis','wiki:Death_of_James_Cook','wiki:Second_voyage_of_James_Cook'],img='File:Captainjamescookportrait.jpg',end=1779),
X('first-opium-war','The Opium Wars and the "century of humiliation"',H,'confirmed',1,1839,['textual'],
 "By the early 19th century British merchants were selling large quantities of opium grown in British India to China, in defiance of a Qing ban, to pay for the tea, silk and porcelain that Britain bought. In 1839 the Daoguang Emperor sent the official Lin Zexu to Guangzhou (Canton) to end the trade; he forced British dealers to hand over about 1,400 tonnes of opium and had it destroyed in public at Humen and wrote an open letter to Queen Victoria. Britain sent a fleet, and its steam-powered warships and modern guns defeated Qing forces. The Treaty of Nanking (1842) ceded Hong Kong Island, opened five ports to British trade and imposed a large indemnity. A Second Opium War (1856–60) ended with British and French troops burning the Old Summer Palace in Beijing.",
 [('Drugs or diplomacy?','The war is widely remembered as a war to force opium on China; some contemporaries and historians, including John Quincy Adams in 1841, argued the real issue was Chinese refusal to trade and negotiate on equal terms. Most historians see opium profits and the demand for open markets as inseparable.'),
  ('The century of humiliation','Twentieth-century Chinese nationalists treated 1839 as the start of a century of humiliation by foreign powers lasting until 1949, and many historians date the beginning of modern Chinese history to the war; the idea remains central to official Chinese historical memory.')],
 ['wiki:First_Opium_War','wiki:Second_Opium_War','wiki:Lin_Zexu'],img='First Opium War',end=1860),
X('great-trek-1836','The Great Trek and the Day of the Vow: history and national myth',H,'confirmed',2,1836,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "From 1836 several thousand Dutch-speaking settlers, Boers who called themselves Voortrekkers, left the British-ruled Cape Colony with their wagons, livestock and servants and moved into the interior of southern Africa, angered by British rule, frontier policies and the end of slavery in 1834. Their migration led to conflict with the Northern Ndebele and the Zulu kingdom. In February 1838 the Zulu king Dingane had the trek leader Piet Retief and about seventy of his party killed at uMgungundlovu, and Zulu forces attacked trekker camps; on 16 December 1838 a trekker commando under Andries Pretorius defeated a Zulu army at the Battle of Blood River (Ncome). The trekkers founded the Natalia Republic and later the South African Republic (Transvaal) and the Orange Free State.",
 [('A covenant with God','Before Blood River the trekkers made a vow that if God granted them victory they would keep the day holy. In the 20th century Afrikaner nationalism made the Great Trek and the vow central to its identity, culminating in the 1938 centenary re-enactment and the Voortrekker Monument (1949); 16 December became the Day of the Vow, renamed the Day of Reconciliation in 1995.'),
  ('Empty land?','A long-standing settler claim held that the trekkers moved into an interior emptied by the Mfecane wars; historians reject this "empty land" idea, showing that the interior was inhabited and that the trek dispossessed African communities.')],
 ['wiki:Great_Trek','wiki:Battle_of_Blood_River','wiki:Day_of_Reconciliation'],img='Voortrekker Monument',end=1852),
]
